---

<Layout title={title} description={description}>

<Fragment slot="head-scripts">
  {schemaJsons.map((schema) => (<script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(schema)} />))}
</Fragment>

<ServiceHero
  section="services"
  slug="freezer-repair/lg-not-freezing"
  eyebrow="LG Freezer Not Freezing · Diagnostic Guide · Same Day Southern California"
  title="LG Freezer Not Freezing? Start With Frost and Coils"
  subtitle="An LG that runs but won't stay frozen is usually the defrost system iced over, dirty coils, the evaporator fan, or the linear compressor. Here's what to check and how the 10-year compressor warranty fits in."
  heroImageTitle="LG Freezer Not Freezing Diagnosis and Repair"
  showBranches={true}
/>

<!-- 01 ANSWER-FIRST -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">01 · The short answer</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">A no-freeze LG is usually defrost, coils, the fan, or the compressor.</h2>

    <p><strong>When an LG freezer runs but won't stay frozen, the causes are a defrost system iced over the evaporator coil, dirty condenser coils, a failed evaporator fan, a bad start relay, or a sealed-system/compressor fault. A freezer should hold about 0°F; when it drifts up into the 20s or 30s and food softens, one of those is why. A thick sheet of frost on the back panel points at defrost; a warm freezer with clean coils and a good seal points deeper. On LG, the linear (or inverter) compressor is the part tied to the cooling story — and it carries LG's long parts warranty, which matters if the compressor is the fault. Cleaning coils and checking the door seal are homeowner-safe; defrost, fan, and sealed-system work is a tech job. $89 residential diagnostic, waived with repair.</strong></p>

    <p class="callout"><strong>Keep the door shut while you wait.</strong> Per USDA guidance, a full freezer holds a safe temperature about 48 hours (24 hours if half-full) if the door stays closed — so a not-freezing LG is worth a same-day call before food is at risk.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 02 DEFROST + LINEAR COMPRESSOR -->
<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">02 · Frost points at defrost; the compressor is the LG story</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Two LG-specific things to know.</h2>

    <p><strong>Frost first.</strong> A frost-free LG runs a defrost cycle that periodically melts frost off the evaporator coil. When the defrost heater, sensor, or control fails, frost builds until it blocks airflow and the freezer can't cool — and it often jams the evaporator fan. So a wall of frost on the back interior panel that keeps returning after you clear it is a defrost fault, not something to just chip away.</p>

    <p><strong>Then the compressor.</strong> If coils, seal, and defrost are ruled out and the freezer still won't cool, the sealed system or the compressor is the fault. On LG that's the linear (or inverter) compressor — and LG's long compressor parts warranty (commonly 10 years) may cover the compressor part itself. It's parts-not-labor and depends on your model and purchase date, so find your paperwork. It's the same compressor story we cover on our <a href="/services/refrigerator-repair/lg-not-cooling/">LG refrigerator not cooling</a> page.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 03 CAUSES -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">03 · Why an LG freezer won't freeze</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">The causes, in the order our techs check them.</h2>

    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Dirty condenser coils (check first)</h3>
      <p>Coils caked in dust can't shed heat, so the freezer slowly loses its ability to hold 0°F. The most common homeowner-fixable cause, and worse in an LA summer.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Defrost system iced over</h3>
      <p>A failed defrost heater, sensor, or control lets frost build on the evaporator until air can't pass. The tell is a thick, returning sheet of frost on the back panel. Tech repair.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Evaporator fan</h3>
      <p>Circulates cold air over the coil through the freezer. If it fails or frost jams it, the freezer warms even though the compressor runs. Tech repair.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Door seal / blocked vents</h3>
      <p>A worn gasket or packed food blocking the interior vents lets the freezer warm. Both are worth checking before assuming a part.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Start relay / linear compressor / sealed system</h3>
      <p>If the compressor won't start or runs without cooling, the relay, the linear compressor, or the sealed system is the fault — the higher-ticket end, and where the LG compressor warranty may apply. A gauges-and-tech diagnosis.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 04 SAFE FIX -->
<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">04 · What you can safely check</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Try these before the food is at risk.</h2>

    <ol class="diag-list">
      <li><strong>Vacuum the condenser coils</strong> behind the rear or under a front kick-plate. This alone fixes a lot of gradual not-freezing cases.</li>
      <li><strong>Check the door seal</strong> closes fully and nothing's holding the door ajar.</li>
      <li><strong>Confirm the temperature setting</strong> is cold enough and the interior vents aren't blocked by packed food.</li>
      <li><strong>Give it 24 hours</strong> after any temperature change or a big grocery load before judging, and note your model and purchase date in case the compressor warranty applies.</li>
    </ol>

    <p class="callout"><strong>Where to stop:</strong> coils, door seal, temperature, and vents are safe. The defrost parts, evaporator/condenser fan, start relay, linear compressor, and sealed system carry shock and refrigerant risks — our techs' work.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 05 SIBLING -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">05 · Standalone or the freezer side of a fridge</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Same diagnosis, whichever LG you have.</h2>

    <p>This walks the same causes whether it's a standalone LG freezer or the freezer compartment of an LG refrigerator — the defrost, fan, coils, and compressor work the same way. If it's your fridge and the freezer's fine but the fresh-food side is warm, that's a different split — see <a href="/services/refrigerator-repair/fridge-not-cooling-freezer-works/">fridge not cooling, freezer works</a>. And the same not-freezing fault on another brand: <a href="/services/freezer-repair/frigidaire-not-freezing/">Frigidaire freezer not freezing</a>, or the general <a href="/services/freezer-repair/not-freezing/">freezer not freezing</a> guide.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 06 TECH + LINKS -->
<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">06 · When to call us</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Coils clean, seal good, still not freezing?</h2>

    <p>When the coils are clean, the door seals, and the LG still won't hold 0°F, the fault is inside — the defrost system, the evaporator fan, the start relay, or the compressor and sealed system. Our techs test each, and if it's the linear compressor, we'll tell you straight whether it's a warranty claim. We carry the common LG defrost parts and fan motors for a one-visit fix where possible.</p>

    <p>More help: the general <a href="/services/freezer-repair/not-freezing/">freezer not freezing</a> guide, <a href="/services/freezer-repair/ice-buildup/">freezer ice buildup</a> if it's mostly frost, the LG compressor story on <a href="/services/refrigerator-repair/lg-not-cooling/">LG refrigerator not cooling</a>, and the sibling <a href="/services/freezer-repair/frigidaire-not-freezing/">Frigidaire not freezing</a>. Same-day across <a href="/los-angeles/freezer-repair/">Los Angeles</a>, <a href="/beverly-hills/freezer-repair/">Beverly Hills</a>, and <a href="/pasadena/freezer-repair/">Pasadena</a> in most cases. <a href="/book/">Book online</a> or call {phone}.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 07 FAQ -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">07 · FAQ</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">LG freezer not freezing: common questions.</h2>

    <div class="faq-list">
      {faqs.map(f => (
        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>{f.q}</summary>
          <p>{f.a}</p>
        </details>
      ))}
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 08 BOTTOM CTA -->
<section class="bottom-cta">
  <div class="container">
    <h2>LG freezer not freezing? Call today — food is on a clock.</h2>
    <p>$89 diagnostic waived with repair. Same-day across LA, OC, Ventura. Phones answered 24/7.</p>
    <div class="cta-row">
      <a href={phoneHref} class="btn-primary">☎ Call {phone}</a>
      <a href="/book/" class="btn-secondary">Book Online</a>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

</Layout>

